<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">French Saint-Barthélemy</h1><p class="st-meta">1648 CE – 1784 CE · capital Le Carénage</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small, dry French island in the Leewards that passed through the hands of the Knights of Malta before France traded it to Sweden in 1784.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Saint Kitts</a>, sent by the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</a>, occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saint-barthelemy-swedish">Saint-Barthélemy</a> in 1648. In 1651 the island was included in the sale of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> islands to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Kalinago</a> attack in 1656 wiped out the settlement, and it was repopulated only slowly, partly by settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Normandy</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Brittany</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lacking water and good soil, the island never became a plantation colony. Its small population of white smallholders lived by fishing, salt, livestock and modest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> growing, with relatively few enslaved people. It was governed as a dependency of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-guadeloupe">Guadeloupe</a> or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Saint Kitts</a> rather than as a full colony, and its harbour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavia">Le Carénage</a>, was the only real settlement. The British occupied it briefly in the 18th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1784 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> ceded the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in exchange for trading rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a>. The Swedes renamed the harbour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavia">Gustavia</a> and made it a free port. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> bought the island back in 1878, and it is now an overseas collectivity of France.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1648 CE – 1784 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><path d="M50 28 L32 56 H50"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">colony</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.50%;width:2.71%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1648 CE – 1784 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Le Carénage</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Philippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Saint Barthélemy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saint-Barthélemy (French)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1716&amp;focus=french-saint-barthelemy" aria-label="French Saint-Barthélemy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2975 1513 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Saint-Barthélemy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M3295 1791h0M3295 1769h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3295 1769h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3295 1769h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Saint Barthélemy. <a href="/atlas?year=1716&amp;focus=french-saint-barthelemy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
